 - Recompute `ceil(remaining current-prompt chapters / days in the rolling planning horizon)` for forecasting, but never let that number override the calibrated operational wave cap in `data/people/campaign-policy.json`. Distinguish deployed `master`, staging current-prompt coverage, and all extracted chapters.
 - Treat the forecast rate as a feasibility signal, not an instruction to create an uncalibrated spending burst. Preserving complete, source-backed data is more important than an arbitrary date. Keep all twelve Grok Bot workers occupied and recycle them immediately after accepted handoff so their separate allowance adds useful throughput.
-- Track editorially closed coverage and identity-clean coverage as separate critical paths. A chapter is editorially closed only when it uses the current prompt and has no proposed translation repairs. It is identity-clean only when none of its canonical people retain unresolved same-name candidates. Size both lanes from their own remaining debt rather than copying the extraction wave size; extraction coverage alone is not glossary completion and cannot pass the ebook publication gate.
+- Track editorial closure and current chapter identity certificates separately. Unresolved same-name candidates do not block a chapter once its own assignments and canonical anchors are independently certified and cross-chapter uncertainty is explicitly reviewed. Concrete attribution or false-merge contradictions still block. Extraction coverage alone cannot pass chapter or ebook completion.
 - Prefer three roughly 50-chapter extraction waves per day over four smaller waves once a model and allowance window are calibrated. This keeps the same daily quota while paying the long-tail drain and review setup costs fewer times. Start a new model or allowance window at concurrency 8-12, then raise to 16-20 only when accepted p90 cost, tokens, duration, and failure rate support it.
 - Use `--order deadline-balanced` for deadline extraction waves. It interleaves three short chapters with one chapter sampled across the remaining workload, continuously draining the expensive tail instead of leaving the largest chapters for the final days.
 - Use the same 3:1 workload shape for September Grok Bot claims: pass `--order deadline-balanced --max-units 80 --max-candidates 200 --max-worker-kib 48`. Stable worker IDs put one in four workers on a window sampled at 45%, 60%, 75%, or 90% through the size-ranked corpus and the rest on the shortest pool. Reserve pathological extremes for Cursor's adaptive parallel chunks or deliberate assignment. A worker's existing sticky claim always takes precedence.
 - In this repository, `data/people/campaign-policy.json` is authoritative for reset calendars: Grok Bot resumes September 13, 2026, while Cursor SDK resumes September 24, 2026. Never collapse them into one shared capacity date. Run `npm run people:extract:deadline-recovery` first, then `npm run people:extract:deadline-wave` only when Cursor is available; use the shared queue's Grok Bot commands independently when that lane is available. Artifact-only recovery remains runnable during a Cursor blackout. The launcher reports the soft-target rate but caps operational wave size and concurrency; follow accepted extraction with `npm run people:editorial:deadline-wave`. Invocation ceilings are circuit breakers, not spending targets.
-- Prefer `npm run people:deadline:day` for a calibrated production day. It checkpoints recovery plus three extraction/editorial/identity cycles and resumes at the first unfinished stage when rerun on the same date. The editorial wave must apply accepted host-side decisions before it counts as closure; `people:editorial:deadline-wave` does this automatically through the batch applier. On the first paid day after a reset, run `npm run people:deadline:calibrate` first and inspect the accepted 10-scope identity cohort before starting the production command.
+- Do not launch historical extraction/editorial/identity day loops while downstream completion is stalled. Use current repository completion stages and retained ownership. Apply accepted host-side editorial corrections before counting closure. A retired pair-wave command must not be restored or used to schedule fresh identity work.
 - The managed daily runner defers each extraction wave's global catalog rebuild and forces one after its paired editorial wave. Keep that coalescing in the daily path: extraction and editorial use the same newly accepted chapter cohort, while identity resolution still starts from a fresh catalog. Standalone wave commands retain immediate rebuild behavior.
-- During a paid-capacity blackout, run `npm run people:resolution:deadline-prepare` and commit the generated dossier directory to staging. Then run `npm run people:resolution:deadline-wave -- --limit 10` as the first post-reset identity calibration before launching any new extraction; the launcher will use the source-verified prepared dossiers instead of rebuilding the full corpus graph. Once accepted cost, duration, effective worker-run count, and validation rates are known, omit the override to use the planner's buffered identity target. Rank identity seeds by compiled comparison workload, not the number of affected canonical pages: select seven cheapest remaining chapter scopes for every scope sampled at the 60th percentile of the remaining workload. Identity components have a much steeper fan-out tail than extraction length, so do not reuse extraction's 3:1 shape. Derive a stable resumable batch ID from the exact selection and refresh the canonical catalog after a complete aggregate is accepted. Partial resolver checkpoints remain attached to that stable batch and must be resumed before a new selection.
+- Fresh identity work uses `people:identity:registry`, `people:identity:prepare`, and the current reviewed record path. Reuse the immutable registry and unchanged proofs. Rank chapters by total remaining completion work, not legacy pair-graph workload. Recover retained old resolver assignments only through their exact saved invocation; never commission fresh pair work. A prepared packet is not an independent approval or a published chapter.
 - Keep bare Chinese offices and sovereign titles such as `皇帝`, `太后`, `太子`, `皇太子`, `皇子`, and `將軍`, plus their generic English forms such as `Crown Prince`, `Imperial Prince`, and `Heir Apparent`, non-blocking in identity candidates. A local person represented only by a generic title is an extraction defect: source-audit it and use `scripts/remove-false-people.mjs` when it is not an individual. This applies especially to ritual treatises whose stage directions repeatedly mention generic participants such as the emperor, crown prince, prefect, guest, assistant, or ceremonial elders. Do not spend an identity-resolution slot trying to merge or separate a dynasty-wide title record.
 - Keep a preferred label non-blocking when every matching source claim identifies it only as a surname. Preserve the surname-only person and their chapter mention, but do not generate cross-corpus identity work from a bare surname such as `Wang`; genuine mononyms supported by personal-name claims remain blocking.
 - Apply the same rule to preferred labels supported only by title, regnal, temple, or posthumous-name claims. A bare epithet such as `惠` must not reopen a dynasty-spanning identity component merely because the extractor selected it as the preferred Chinese label; stronger personal-name and full-title evidence still blocks normally.
 - Match the schema's concrete `title-name`, `title-reference`, `regnal-name`, `temple-name`, and `posthumous-name` values when enforcing that rule. Keep generic surname-clan labels such as `Lady Wang` / `王氏` and bare kinship labels such as `His Wife`, `Third Son`, `其妻`, and `第三子` non-blocking, while preserving specific descriptions such as `Wife of Zhang Wenxu`. A one-word English reading paired with Chinese must not create a separate candidate key: compare `Zhong 仲` and `Zhong 忠` through their Chinese forms, while preserving exact Chinese mononyms as legitimate candidate evidence. Partition a shared Romanized English name only when every shared English form is supported as an identity name and both records have nonempty, disjoint sets of specific Chinese identity names; preserve the comparison if either side lacks that evidence. Measure resolver throughput by remaining `reviewPairs`, not raw block count: on September 9 this partition removed 10,961 live pairs and 948 unresolved blocks.
 - Filter generic relationship surfaces by exact normalized value, including `Wife`, `Younger Brother`, and `妻子`; keep specific identities such as `Wife of Zhang Wenxu` eligible. In large famous-person blocks, use one evidence-supported multi-person merge to connect the compatible canonical groups rather than emitting a quadratic list of pair decisions.
-- Prepared dossiers are staging-only coordination artifacts. Keep them while any shard or part remains incomplete; after the aggregate is accepted and the catalog rebuild succeeds, remove that batch directory in the same resolution checkpoint commit. Never carry completed dossier payloads into a squash merge to `master`.
+- Keep sealed chapter review packets, original reports and resumable artifacts until their work is accepted and recoverable. Do not delete persistent canonical dossiers as if they were disposable legacy batch payloads. Follow repository rules for selective recovery checkpoints and tracked publication files.
 - For the September 2026 deadline campaign, pilot fresh chapters at 80 owned units and 200 candidates while retaining the 48 KiB packet ceiling. Keep prior adaptive chunk plans unchanged. Fall back to 60/150 if the pilot's accepted p90 or validation-failure rate worsens materially.
 - Use a 20-minute remote run timeout for extraction after calibration. Preserve the sticky conversation and accepted chunks, then split or resume only the missing ownership range. A stalled run must not hold a production slot for the SDK's generic 45-minute default.
 - Treat Cursor `usage_limit_exceeded` as an account-wide capacity stop, not a chapter failure. Stop all new extraction and editorial launches immediately, let active work drain, preserve accepted chunks and conversations, and resume those sticky assignments before opening fresh chapters when capacity returns.
